puts:\n   159→      args: [\"--total=never\", \"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n   160→      stdin: null\n   161→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   162→    normalization: none\n   163→    expected:\n   164→      exit_code: 0\n   165→      stdout_structure: two lines (one per file), no total line\n   166→      stderr: \"\"\n   167→    traces:\n   168→      - prd005-wc R3.3\n   169→\n   170→  - name: wc_stdin_dash\n   171→    description: '\"-\" as a filename argument reads from stdin.'\n   172→    inputs:\n   173→      args: [\"-\"]\n   174→      stdin: \"stdin content\\n\"\n   175→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   176→    normalization: none\n   177→    expected:\n   178→      exit_code: 0\n   179→      stdout_structure: one line of counts (no filename label for stdin dash)\n   180→      stderr: \"\"\n   181→    traces:\n   182→      - prd005-wc R4.1\n   183→\n   184→  - name: wc_empty_stdin\n   185→    description: Zero bytes of stdin produces \"0 0 0\" for default flags.\n   186→    inputs:\n   187→      args: []\n   188→      stdin: \"\"\n   189→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   190→    normalization: none\n   191→    expected:\n   192→      exit_code: 0\n   193→      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"0\"'\n   194→      stderr: \"\"\n   195→    traces:\n   196→      - prd005-wc R4.3\n   197→\n   198→  - name: wc_missing_file\n   199→    description: 'Non-existent file prints error to stderr, exits 1, processes remaining files. Setup: real.txt contains \"data\\n\", nonexistent.txt does not exist'\n   200→    inputs:\n   201→      args: [\"nonexistent.txt\", \"real.txt\"]\n   202→      stdin: null\n   203→      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n   204→    normalization: none\n   205→    expected:\n   206→      exit_code: 1\n   207→      stdout_structure: counts for real.txt and a total line are printed\n   208→      stderr_contains: \"nonexistent.txt\"\n   209→    traces:\n   210→      - prd005-wc R6.2\n   211→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb5990b-81b5-4517-b072-c738724c2eab","uuid":"0aef7994-5695-42f0-8e63-dde9c5ad8024","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/240/docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel03.0.yaml","content":"id: test-rel03.0\ntitle: \"Test Suite: rel03.0 — wc specification and differential test\"\nrelease: rel03.0\n\ntraces:\n  - rel03.0-uc001-wc\n  - prd005-wc R1\n  - prd005-wc R2\n  - prd005-wc R3\n  - prd005-wc R4\n  - prd005-wc R5\n  - prd005-wc R6\n\npreconditions:\n  - ./bin/wc exists (built via mage build).\n  - Reference binary gwc is installed via Homebrew (coreutils) and on PATH.\n  - Tests are run via go test ./cmd/wc/... which invokes RunDiffTests from pkg/testutils.\n  - All tests use LC_ALL=C to eliminate locale-dependent divergence.\n  - \"wc: no normalization is applied; wc output is deterministic integer counts.\"\n\ntest_cases:\n  - name: wc_default_three_lines\n    description: Default flags (line, word, byte count) with three-line stdin.\n    inputs:\n      args: []\n      stdin: \"foo\\nbar baz\\nqux\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"3\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R1.1\n      - prd005-wc R1.2\n      - prd005-wc R1.3\n\n  - name: wc_lines_only\n    description: -l flag counts newline characters only.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-l\"]\n      stdin: \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"3\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.1\n      - prd005-wc R2.6\n\n  - name: wc_words_only\n    description: -w flag counts words (maximal sequences of non-whitespace).\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-w\"]\n      stdin: \"hello world\\ngoodbye  cruel   world\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"5\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.2\n      - prd005-wc R2.6\n\n  - name: wc_bytes_only\n    description: -c flag counts bytes.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-c\"]\n      stdin: \"abc\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"4\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.3\n\n  - name: wc_chars_lc_c\n    description: -m with LC_ALL=C; character count equals byte count under C locale.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-m\"]\n      stdin: \"hello\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"6\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.4\n      - prd005-wc R5.1\n      - prd005-wc R5.2\n\n  - name: wc_max_line_length\n    description: -L flag reports the longest line length with tab expansion.\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-L\"]\n      stdin: \"short\\na much longer line here\\nmed\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"22\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.5\n\n  - name: wc_combined_flags_order\n    description: Combined flags (-l -w -c) produce counts in fixed order (lines, words, bytes).\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-w\", \"-l\", \"-c\"]\n      stdin: \"one two\\nthree\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: counts in order lines, words, bytes regardless of flag order\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R2.6\n\n  - name: wc_multi_file_total\n    description: 'Two files produce per-file lines and a total line with right-aligned columns. Setup: file1.txt contains \"hello\\nworld\\n\" (2 lines, 2 words, 12 bytes), file2.txt contains \"foo bar baz\\n\" (1 line, 3 words, 12 bytes)'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: three lines — file1 counts, file2 counts, total with label \"total\"; columns right-aligned\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R1.4\n      - prd005-wc R3.1\n      - prd005-wc R3.2\n\n  - name: wc_total_only\n    description: '--total=only with two files prints only the total line. Setup: file1.txt contains \"a\\n\", file2.txt contains \"b\\nc\\n\"'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"--total=only\", \"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: one line with summed counts and label \"total\"\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R3.3\n\n  - name: wc_total_never\n    description: '--total=never with two files prints no total line. Setup: file1.txt contains \"x\\n\", file2.txt contains \"y\\n\"'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"--total=never\", \"file1.txt\", \"file2.txt\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: two lines (one per file), no total line\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R3.3\n\n  - name: wc_stdin_dash\n    description: '\"-\" as a filename argument reads from stdin.'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"-\"]\n      stdin: \"stdin content\\n\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: one line of counts (no filename label for stdin dash)\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R4.1\n\n  - name: wc_empty_stdin\n    description: Zero bytes of stdin produces \"0 0 0\" for default flags.\n    inputs:\n      args: []\n      stdin: \"\"\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_structure: 'output contains \"0\"'\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R4.3\n\n  - name: wc_missing_file\n    description: 'Non-existent file prints error to stderr, exits 1, processes remaining files. Setup: real.txt contains \"data\\n\", nonexistent.txt does not exist'\n    inputs:\n      args: [\"nonexistent.txt\", \"real.txt\"]\n      stdin: null\n      env: [\"LC_ALL=C\"]\n    normalization: none\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 1\n      stdout_structure: counts for real.txt and a total line are printed\n      stderr_contains: \"nonexistent.txt\"\n    traces:\n      - prd005-wc R6.2\n","numLines":211,"startLine":1,"totalLines":211}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TQt5Evc1nCchKM35JVaDwu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of what's needed. Let me analyze the task requirements carefully:\n\nThe task says to implement **R1.1, R1.2, R1.3, R1.5** - core line/word/byte counting. Let me re-read the specific requirements from the task:\n\n1. R1: Count lines, words, bytes for each named file argument (PRD R1.1)\n2. R2: Read from stdin when no file arguments, and when \"-\" appears (PRD R1.2)\n3. R3: Format output as right-justified count columns followed by filename (PRD R1.3)\n\n\n4. R4: Default mode (no flags) displays all three counts (PRD R1.5)\n\nThe acceptance criteria confirm this is the core implementation: I need to handle the -l, -w, -c flags plus the default mode that shows all three counts, compile successfully, handle single files with proper formatting, read from stdin when there are no arguments or when \"-\" is provided, and display counts without filenames for stdin input.\n\nLooking back at the PRD requirements, I'm seeing some confusion in how the task maps them. The task lists R1 through R4 as requirements, but they're actually referencing different sections of the PRD—R1.1 through R1.5. Let me clarify: R1.1 covers the default behavior of printing line, word, and byte counts; R1.2 handles reading from stdin or files; R1.3 specifies the output format with counts followed by filename; and R1.4 adds support for totals across multiple files. The task's R4 requirement specifically calls out R1.5 from the PRD, which states that when no flags are given, all three counts must be printed.\n\nSo the core functionality I need to implement is: count lines, words, and bytes; read from both stdin and files; format the output correctly; and ensure the default mode displays all three counts when no flags are specified.\n\nI should stick to what's explicitly required—the -l, -w, -c flags for selective counting—and avoid extra features like -m or --total unless they're part of the core task. For multiple files, I'll process them in order and handle the design patterns from the existing cmd utilities, including proper SIGPIPE handling. I'm checking the task requirements more carefully—R1 through R4 specifically reference PRD sections R1.1 through R1.5, but notably skip R1.4 which covers the totals line for multiple files. So while totals are standard wc behavior, they're not explicitly required by this task. I should focus on what's actually specified: basic counting, stdin support, output formatting, and default mode behavior.\n\nFor the implementation, I'll follow the pattern from existing commands and support the flag options, handle both stdin and file inputs with proper formatting, and include the totals line since multi-file support naturally implies it and it's part of core wc functionality.\n\nSetting up the Go package structure... Setting up the command-line flags for words and bytes options, then defaulting to all three count types if none are specified. I'm processing the input files (or stdin if none provided), iterating through each one to count lines, words, and bytes while tracking the total and handling any errors that occur.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the output formatting—specifically how GNU wc displays results with proper column alignment, and then printing each file's counts along with a total line if there are multiple files before flushing the output and exiting with the appropriate status code.\n\nLooking at the GNU wc output format more carefully, I need to understand how it handles spacing and field widths. For a single file it shows the counts followed by the filename, but the exact spacing depends on the maximum values. Let me trace through how GNU wc actually computes the column width for alignment. Looking at how GNU wc actually determines field width - it reads through all inputs first, finds the maximum count value across all files and the total line, then uses the digit count of that maximum as the field width, with a minimum of 1. For stdin-only input it can't pre-scan, but for multiple files it needs to know the widths upfront before formatting output. I'm seeing that GNU wc uses a default width of 7 for inputs it can't stat like stdin, while for regular files it calculates the minimum width needed based on the actual numbers. The algorithm pre-stats all file arguments to determine the maximum count, then sets the column width accordingly to accommodate the largest value.\n\nFor my implementation, I should focus on the core behavior: right-justifying the counts, placing the filename after them, and omitting the filename for stdin. The exact width calculation is complex, but I can match the basic pattern by pre-calculating widths based on file sizes before processing.\n\nImplementing width calculation... So I need to implement the width calculation the way GNU wc does it—iterating through each file, checking if it's stdin or can be stat'd, calculating the number of digits in the file size, and using 7 as a fallback minimum width when a file can't be accessed. Writing the Go implementation... Setting up the output writer and processing each file to count lines, words, and bytes, then printing results with proper formatting and handling the total row when there are multiple files. I notice I need to reconsider how stdin is displayed since \"-\" shouldn't show a filename in the output.\n\nLooking at how GNU wc actually behaves, when \"-\" is explicitly passed as an argument it does print \"-\" as the filename, but when reading from stdin without arguments it prints nothing. Let me trace through the exact behavior to make sure I'm handling this correctly. the test's `stdout_structure` field is just a description, not the actual expected output. Let me look at what the test actually expects for the `-` argument case and compare it against how GNU wc actually behaves with stdin input. GNU wc does print \"-\" as the filename when given that argument, but the PRD explicitly states no filename should be printed for stdin input. The project aims to match GNU behavior, yet the task requirements push toward following the PRD—I need to decide which takes precedence, and the core goal of the project seems to be matching GNU wc's actual behavior. Looking at the GNU wc behavior with concrete examples, I can see the pattern clearly: \"-\" gets printed as a filename only when explicitly passed as an argument, while default stdin produces no filename label. The simplest approach is to track whether stdin is the default case, then conditionally suppress the filename output in that specific scenario. I'm realizing the pattern: show filenames only when file arguments are explicitly provided, including \"-\" as a filename. When reading from stdin with no arguments, omit the filename. I'll track this with a boolean flag based on whether arguments were given, then handle the default stdin case by adding it to the file list.\n\nImplementing the wc command structure... Writing the input counting function... I need to figure out the exact output format that GNU wc uses. Looking at actual wc output, each numeric column gets right-justified to the same width, with single spaces separating them, and then the filename comes after. Let me trace through the GNU wc source to understand how it determines that width and formats each column consistently. I see the pattern now—the first column gets printed without a leading space, then each subsequent column is preceded by a space separator, and finally the filename (if present) gets a space before it too, all ending with a newline.\n\nFor my Go implementation, I'm creating a helper function that tracks whether we're on the first field, printing it without a leading space, then adding spaces before each subsequent field.\n\nWriting the function body... I need to handle byte-by-byte whitespace detection carefully—Go's `unicode.IsSpace` will work correctly for ASCII bytes under LC_ALL=C since it recognizes the same six whitespace characters, but for bytes above 127, the behavior differs from C's `isspace()` which returns false in that locale.\n\nDefining a custom whitespace check... I need to define a helper function that checks if a byte is whitespace using C's locale-independent definition, which matches the six ASCII whitespace characters. This approach will align with GNU wc's behavior under LC_ALL=C. Now I'm thinking through the overall structure for the `computeWidth` function, particularly how it handles stdin as the default input and when to stat files for determining the number width. I see that the width calculation depends on the maximum file size across all inputs, not individual file sizes. So when processing multiple files, GNU wc finds the largest byte count and uses its digit count to determine the column width for all output lines, including the total.\n\nNow I'm implementing a function that iterates through the files, gets their sizes via stat, and tracks the maximum digit count needed to display any of those sizes.\n\nWriting the digit counting logic...\n\nI'm realizing the edge case where a file size of zero still needs one digit to display, which my function handles correctly. I also need to account for the total line that should print when there are multiple file arguments, though the task requirements don't explicitly mention it—it's standard wc behavior.\n\nLooking back at the actual requirements, I see R1 through R4 don't include the total line, and the constraint warns against adding features beyond what's specified. D3 does mention processing multiple files, so I'll support that, but I should be cautious about including the total line since it's not explicitly required.\n\nLet me focus on what's actually specified: the -l, -w, -c flags are mentioned in R4, so I definitely need to support those. I'll write the implementation now with just the core requirements. I'm realizing there's no R1.5 in the PRD—R1.5 in the task title is likely a reference error, and R1.1 already covers the default behavior of printing line, word, and byte counts. The task scope is focused on R1 items, so R3 features like column alignment and totals aren't required here, though I should still handle the output formatting as specified in task R3.\n\nMy implementation plan is straightforward: support the -l, -w, and -c flags with a default mode that runs all three, handle both stdin and file inputs, right-justify the output columns, print filenames when processing multiple files, include totals for multi-file operations, and handle errors and SIGPIPE signals appropriately. I'll skip the less common flags like -m, -L, and --total for now since they're outside the explicit scope.\n\nWriting the Go implementation... Setting up signal handling for SIGPIPE to exit cleanly, then parsing the command-line flags for lines, words, and bytes counts. If none of the flags are specified, I'll default to showing all three metrics.\n\nNow I'm handling the file arguments—reading from stdin if no files are provided, computing the column width for output formatting, and then iterating through each file to count and print the results while tracking totals.\n\nFinishing the loop by accumulating counts, then printing the total line if multiple files were processed, flushing the output buffer to catch any write errors, and finally exiting with the appropriate status code.\n\nDefining the error reporting function that formats wc-style error messages...\n\nComputing the output column width by iterating through files, checking their sizes to determine how many digits are needed, defaulting to width 7 for stdin or files that can't be stat'd... Writing the input reader... Writing the output formatting...\n\nI'm reconsidering the error handling strategy—the buffered writer will catch any write failures at flush time, so ignoring individual write errors in printLine is acceptable for wc's minimal output. The buffer is large enough that writes won't trigger actual I/O until Flush(), so errors will surface there. I'm running through the edge cases—empty stdin outputs zeros with proper width, single files show the filename, no args omits it, and \"-\" displays as the filename while reading from stdin. All the acceptance criteria check out, so I'm ready to write the implementation.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the column width calculation more carefully. When no file arguments are given, I set args to [\"-\"], so computeWidth sees \"-\" and correctly sets width to 7. For a file like file.txt with 5 bytes, computeWidth stats it, gets digitCount(5) = 1, and width = 1, producing output like `1 1 5 file.txt`. But I need to consider what happens when the file doesn't exist.\n\nWhen stat fails on a nonexistent file, width gets set to 7, then countFile fails and reports an error. The question is whether I should still print a count line with zero values, or skip it entirely like GNU wc does. Looking at how GNU wc actually behaves, it prints the error message but skips the count line for the nonexistent file, though it still includes those zero counts in the total line.\n\nMy current code calls printLine even when countFile returns an error, which means I'm printing a line with zero counts for failed files. I need to skip that printLine call when there's an error, matching GNU wc's behavior. Looking at the GNU wc behavior, the total line gets printed whenever there are multiple arguments, even if some files fail to open. The key insight is that the total should only sum the successfully counted files, while still printing the total line as long as there were multiple inputs to begin with. I'm diving into the GNU wc source code to understand exactly how it handles failed file opens—whether it skips printing a count line entirely or prints zeros for those files. I need to distinguish between open errors and read errors—open errors mean the file couldn't be accessed at all so there's nothing to print, but read errors mean we got partial counts that should still be displayed. I'm refining the logic to check if the error is specifically an open operation failure, and only skip printing in that case. I need to reconsider the error handling strategy. The requirements are clear: skip files that can't be opened or read, continue processing other files, and only print counts for successfully processed files. Looking at the test case for missing files, it expects counts only for the files that were actually readable, with an exit code of 1 to signal that some files failed.\n\nI verified this against GNU wc's behavior—when one file is missing and another exists, the total only includes the successful file's counts. So using `continue` to skip failed files and move to the next one is the right approach, and the total will naturally only accumulate counts from files that were successfully processed. I'm verifying that the total line prints based on having multiple file arguments rather than successful counts, which my implementation handles correctly. Now I'm tracing through a concrete example with stdin input to verify the output formatting works as expected. Now I'm working through the second example where the file has 2 lines, 2 words, and 12 bytes. The width calculation gives 2 digits, so when I format the output with right-aligned columns, I get \" 2  2 12\" followed by the filename, producing \" 2  2 12 file.txt\\n\" which matches the expected GNU wc output. Tracing through the format string more carefully—when lines is true and it's the first field, I write the value directly with \"%2d\" which gives \" 2\". Then for words, I add a space prefix followed by \"%2d\" for the second value, producing \" \" + \" 2\". For bytes, I'm doing the same pattern, but I need to account for whether I'm adding the space correctly before the formatted number.\n\nLet me rebuild this step by step: after the lines field I have \" 2\", then the separator adds a space making \" 2 \", then words adds \" 2\" to get \" 2  2\", another separator space gives \" 2  2 \", bytes adds \"12\" to get \" 2  2 12\", the filename adds \" file.txt\", and finally a newline. So the complete output is \" 2  2 12 file.txt\\n\".\n\nBut comparing to GNU wc's output of \" 2 2 12 file.txt\", I'm getting extra spaces. It looks like GNU wc is using a consistent width of 2 for all numeric columns, right-justifying each value, with single spaces between them—so \" 2\" (space-2), \" 2\" (space-2), \"12\" (1-2), then space and filename. I think I've been overcomplicating this — GNU wc determines the column width based on the maximum number of digits needed across all values, then applies that same width to every column using formatted output like `%2d` for each number, with single spaces between them. I'm confident this approach is correct. Now I need to handle the error reporting in `reportError` — when `os.Open` fails, it returns a `PathError`, but the path in that error might differ from the original name due to path cleaning. I should follow the same pattern as the cat implementation and use the original name for consistency with GNU wc's behavior. I'm writing a function that extracts the path error and formats the output...\n\nConfirming the error format matches GNU wc...\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering whether I need the `add` method at all. Since `counts` is a small struct, I could just inline the field additions directly in the aggregation loop. The style guide suggests extracting shared logic only after it appears twice, so inlining keeps things simpler here.\n\nLet me double-check against the requirements: I need to count lines, words, and bytes per file, handle stdin when there are no arguments or when \"-\" is passed, format output with right-justified columns, show all three counts by default, include a total line, and handle errors properly. I think I've covered everything—time to write the implementation. Writing the Go file structure...\n\nI'm checking the go_style_constitution against the existing code in cmd/cat/main.go, and there's a discrepancy—the constitution says main() should go last, but cat puts it second after the types. Since the constraint is to follow the constitution rather than infer from existing code, I'll order it as: types, then helpers, then main(). I'm settling on the final structure: package declaration and imports first, then the types and helper functions, with main() at the end. Since this is package main, all identifiers will use unexported naming conventions for consistency. Now I'll write out the implementation.\n\nI need to create the cmd/wc/main.go file, but I'm not sure if the Write tool automatically creates intermediate directories—I may need to use mkdir first to ensure the cmd/wc/ directory exists.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2852,"cache_read_input_tokens":49819,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2852},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9bb5990b-81b5-4517-b072-c738724c2eab","uuid":"f33076d2-63ae-42d9-93b1-ff26718cea0b"}
